# CHAPTER 6 · MARKS, JUMPS, BUFFERS, WINDOWS

Editing speed is half the game. The other half is never being lost:
jumping between definition and call site, config and code, this file
and the one you had open a minute ago — without thinking.

## 6.1 MARKS: BOOKMARKS THAT FOLLOW THE TEXT

`m{a-z}`{normal} drops a mark in this file. `m{A-Z}`{normal} drops a
GLOBAL mark — it works across files (mA in your VHDL entity, mB in
the testbench, bounce between them forever).

`'a`{normal} (apostrophe + letter) jumps to the marked line. The
backtick variant — backtick + letter — jumps to the exact column,
but backtick is a dead key on a Norwegian layout, so make
`'a`{normal} the automatic one. The most useful marks come built in:

- `''`{normal} — back to where you were before the last jump (press
  it twice to bounce between two spots).
- `'.`{normal} — jump to the LAST CHANGE. You scrolled off to read
  something and want to resume editing: this is the key.
- `gi`{normal} — even stronger: jump to the last insert point AND
  enter insert mode.

EXERCISE 1: Drop a mark on the line below with `ma`{normal}, jump to
the top of the file with `gg`{normal}, then come back with
`'a`{normal} and append with `A`{normal} a space and (visited), then
`<Esc>`{normal}:

waypoint alpha -- mark me with ma

EXERCISE 2: On the line below: `A`{normal}, a space, the word draft,
`<Esc>`{normal}. Now jump away with `gg`{normal}. Then press
`gi`{normal} — you are back in insert mode exactly where you left —
and type a space and v2, then `<Esc>`{normal}:

notes:

## 6.2 THE JUMPLIST AND CHANGELIST

Every "big" jump (`gg`{normal}, `G`{normal}, `/`{normal},
`%`{normal}, `gd`{normal}...) is recorded. `<C-o>`{normal} walks back
through them, `<C-i>`{normal} forward — browser back/forward for
code. This works ACROSS FILES: chase a definition three files deep
with `gd`{normal}, then `<C-o>`{normal} `<C-o>`{normal} `<C-o>`{normal}
home. `g;`{normal} / `g,`{normal} do the same for CHANGE locations.

Drill here: `gg`{normal}, `G`{normal}, `50%`{normal}, then
`<C-o>`{normal} back through all of it, `<C-i>`{normal} forward again.

## 6.3 BUFFERS: THE REAL TABS

Every opened file stays loaded as a buffer. Your keys:
`<Tab>`{normal} / `<S-Tab>`{normal} cycle buffers, `<leader>x`{normal}
closes one, `<leader>b`{normal} opens an empty one. Beyond cycling:

- `<C-^>`{normal} — alternate file: flips between the two buffers you
  use most. The single highest-value navigation key in vim.
- `:b partial_name`{vim} — jumps to the buffer matching the text
  (tab-completes). Faster than cycling once you have 10 files open.
- `<leader>fb`{normal} — Telescope buffer picker, fuzzy.

## 6.4 WINDOWS

`<C-w>s`{normal} / `<C-w>v`{normal} split horizontal/vertical (or
`:sp`{vim} / `:vsp`{vim}). NvChad gives you `<C-h>`{normal}
`<C-j>`{normal} `<C-k>`{normal} `<C-l>`{normal} to hop between
windows directly. `<C-w>q`{normal} closes one, `<C-w>o`{normal}
closes all OTHERS, `<C-w>=`{normal} equalises sizes.

Workflow worth stealing: code left, its testbench right —
`:vsp`{vim}, `<C-l>`{normal}, `:b tb_`{vim}<Tab>. Marks, jumplist and
`<C-^>`{normal} all work per-window.

## 6.5 QUICKFIX: A TODO LIST OF LOCATIONS

The quickfix list is vim's "list of places": compile errors, grep
hits, LSP references all land there. `:copen`{vim} shows it,
`:cnext`{vim} / `:cprev`{vim} walk it, `<Enter>`{normal} on an entry
jumps. In chapter 8 you will fill it from Telescope with one key.

## WORKOUT

In your real config repo: open three files, weave between them using
ONLY `<C-^>`{normal}, `:b name`{vim} and `<C-o>`{normal} — no file
tree, no buffer cycling. Drop mA / mB global marks in two of them
and bounce. Notice how rarely you actually need `<leader>e`{normal}.

